n":["[2,8,5,7,3,4,1,6]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[8,10,8,3,9,11,4,11,9,3,4]"],"out":"[8,11,9,3,4]","g":1},{"in":["[4,1,2,3]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[15,6,9,7,21,2,20,16,5,25,28,18,10,8,13,19,1,27,12,14,17,4,22,11,24,26,3,23]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[7,11,5,3,1,11,7,1,4,5,4]"],"out":"[11,7,1,5,4]","g":1},{"in":["[6,7,5,6,1,7,1]"],"out":"[6,7,1]","g":1},{"in":["[4,3,8,1,6,5,6,11,8,9,7]"],"out":"[6,8]","g":1},{"in":["[2,5,10,6,9,4,1,3,7,8]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[6,2,6,4,3,2,3,4,1,1]"],"out":"[6,2,3,4,1]","g":1},{"in":["[11,9,11,8,2,3,10,1,9,5,6]"],"out":"[11,9]","g":1},{"in":["[7,3,3,10,8,15,6,9,16,14,15,1,2,8,16,5]"],"out":"[3,15,8,16]","g":1},{"in":["[2,2,3]"],"out":"[2]","g":1},{"in":["[4,3,2,1]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[7,5,7,8,1,3,3,4]"],"out":"[7,3]","g":1},{"in":["[2,1]"],"out":"[]","g":1},{"in":["[3,9,2,3,7,2,7,9,6,6]"],"out":"[3,2,7,9,6]","g":1},{"in":["[4,4,6,1,2,2,6]"],"out":"[4,2,6]","g":1},{"in":["[6,8,10,4,9,8,10,6,4,9,1]"],"out":"[8,10,6,4,9]","g":1},{"in":[],"out":"\"#235546:89ce648f4c077bd6\"","perf":{"label":"n = 100,000, 40,000 values twice","gen":"int[] p = BCB.perm(100000, 1); int[] a0 = new int[100000]; for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++) a0[i] = p[i < 60000 ? i : i - 40000]; for (int i = a0.length - 1; i > 0; i--) { int j = BCB.R.nextInt(i + 1); int t = a0[i]; a0[i] = a0[j]; a0[j] = t; }"},"refMs":28}],"lesson":"cyclic-sort","statement":"`nums` has `n` integers, each between `1` and `n`, and every value appears either once or twice. Return all the values that appear twice, in any order.\n\nYour algorithm must run in O(n) time and use only constant extra space (the returned list does not count).","constraints":["n == nums.length","1 <= n <= 1e5","1 <= nums[i] <= n","Each value appears once or twice"],"hints":["A HashSet finds repeats in O(n) time but O(n) space. The values are indexes in disguise: value v can be tied to index v − 1.","Cyclic sort: swap each value into index v − 1, skipping a value whose home already holds the same value. Then a slot i holding something other than i + 1 holds a spare copy, a duplicate.","Or sign marking: for each v, look at nums[|v| − 1]. If it is already negative, |v| was seen before, so it is a duplicate; otherwise negate it."],"explain":"In `[4,3,2,7,8,2,3,1]` the values 2 and 3 each appear twice and every other value appears once, so the answer is `[2,3]` (in any order)."},
